 "title": "Expanding single brackets",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "Multiply everything inside the bracket by the term outside: 3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15.",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "To expand a bracket, multiply each thing inside by the number outside. 3(x + 4) = 3 × x + 3 × 4 = 3x + 12.",
   "Think of 3 bags, each holding x sweets plus 4 more sweets: altogether you have 3x + 12 sweets."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "Expanding uses the distributive law: a(b + c) = ab + ac. So 3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15, and 4(x − 2) = 4x − 8.",
   "The most common slip is multiplying only the first term: 3(x + 4) is not 3x + 4. With a negative outside, every sign inside flips: −2(x − 3) = −2x + 6."
  ]
 },
